+### The free route's success rate, stated as two denominators
+
+**No single success rate for this route is defensible, and none is recorded
+here.** The figures that were in circulation — "27 sends, 19 success, 8 failed"
+— reproduce from no committed evidence and have no denominator a reader can
+check. Two denominators are real, and they measure different things, so they are
+recorded separately and are **not** averaged into a third:
+
+| denominator | what it is | outcome split |
+|---|---|---|
+| **217 distinct receipts** across all of `reports/evidence/` | every settled receipt identity the tree commits, deduplicated across the 383 rows that repeat them | 181 success, 11 failure, 25 unknown |
+| **15 B-lane operations** (`invr1b11-budget`, `invr1b12-swe`, `invr1b17-budgetfit`) | the milestone-B live sends only | 8 success, 4 failure, 3 unknown |
+| **9 of those at `max_output_tokens: 2048`** | the same B-lane operations restricted to the budget every campaign pins | 4 success, 4 failure, 1 unknown — **0.444** |
+
+The first row spans studies that ran at different output budgets, different
+deadlines and different periods, so pooling it describes the archive rather
+than the route under any one protocol. The second and third are the ones a cap
+sheet can be priced against, and **they disagree with each other** — 0.533
+against 0.444 — which is itself the finding: excluding B11's five successes at
+16/64/256/1024/2048 removes the only rungs below 2048 and makes the route look
+markedly worse. B17's fourth operation (`a1`, `elapsed_s: 0.6`,
+`exception_class: PreGatewayRefusal`) is excluded from the 15 because it was
+refused before the wire and spent no send.
+
+**What the committed evidence does and does not say about latency.** Every
+measured 502 in the tree carries a latency, and **none is under 120 s**: the
+three recorded values are **180.1 s** and **180.1 s** (the same probe under two
+read ceilings, `invl02-live/probe-chat-180000.json` and
+`probe-responses-300000.json`) and **123.86 s** (`invr1b17-budgetfit`). The
+slowest successful send anywhere in the evidence is **112.57 s**. So the
+ordering holds — every 502 in the tree is slower than every success — but it
+rests on **three** observations, not four, and the four figures circulating
+(`122.7`, `122.7`, `180.6`, `180.8`) appear in no committed file. `b12-swe.md`
+narrates one send lost at **61.4 s**, which is below 120 s and is a timeout
+rather than a 502, so it does not join the 502 set.
+
+**"Route latency, not token budget" is true at 2048 and false at 4096, and
+these are two instruments.** B11's ladder served 16, 64, 256, 1024 and 2048
+with `stop_reason: length` and lost **4096 to a read timeout**, on the same
+prompt. Within 2048 the failures are slow successes that died at the provider
+(123.86 s against a 300 s deadline), which is a latency fact; at 4096 the
+failure is the budget having been spent, which is a budget fact. Averaging
+across the ladder or pooling 4096 with 2048 produces a number that belongs to
+neither budget, so neither is done here.
+
